REGENT OF SLEMAN,

Considering:
a. that to restore the dignity and honor of women and children victims of violence and to restore their social function, it is necessary to undertake protection efforts, women's empowerment, and rehabilitation of children victims of violence;
b. that based on Article 12 of Law 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government, women's empowerment and child protection are mandatory governmental affairs for regional governments;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 15 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 44);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606) as amended by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1 of 2016 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as last amended by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
6. Government Regulation Number 32 of 1950 concerning Determination of the Commencement of Laws Number 12, 13, 14 and 15 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions in East/Central/West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 59);
7. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);
8. Regional Regulation of the Special Region of Yogyakarta Province Number 3 of 2012 concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence (Regional Gazette of the Special Region of Yogyakarta Province of 2012 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of the Special Region of Yogyakarta Province of 2012 Number 3);
9. Regional Regulation of Sleman Regency Number 18 of 2013 concerning Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette of Sleman Regency of 2013 Number 4 Series E, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Sleman Regency Number 79);

With Mutual Approval of

Article 3

Protection of women and children victims of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. gender responsiveness;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining privacy and confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of safety and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitivity to the background and condition of victims;
j. use of appropriate language understood by victims; k. fast and simple;
l. empathy; andm. best interests of victims.

Article 6

1. Prevention of violence against women as referred to in Article 5 letter a is carried out in an integrated manner coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
2. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by involving:a. families;
b. communities;
c. private sector;
d. community institutions;
e. social institutions; andf. educational institutions.

Article 8

1. Protection services for women as referred to in Article 5 letter b are provided to women residents of the Region and/or women who experience violence with the location of the incident in the Regional territory.
2. The implementation of protection services for women is carried out with the principles of:a. fast;
b. safe;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible; andf. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 9

1. Forms of protection services for women victims of violence include: a. legal aid services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. repatriation and social reintegration services;
e. complaint, consultation, and counseling services; and/or f. assistance services.
2. Protection services for women as referred to in paragraph (1) include receiving and sending case referrals from or to other service units in a networked manner.
3. The implementation of protection services for women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment.
4. Further provisions regarding the forms and procedures for protection services for women victims of violence are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 10

1. Empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in Article 5 letter c is implemented through efforts including:a. job training; andb. productive economic enterprises and joint business groups.
2. The implementation of empowerment of women victims of violence is carried out with the principles of:a. equality of rights;
b. responsibility;
c. efficiency and effectiveness; andd. respect and enforcement of human rights.
3. The implementation of empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by related Regional Apparatus in accordance with their main duties and functions.

Article 11

The stages of protection of children victims of violence include: a. prevention of violence against children;
b. handling of children victims of violence; andc. rehabilitation of children victims of violence.

Article 13

Efforts to prevent violence against children in addition to being implemented by regional apparatus as referred to in Article 12 by involving: a. parents and/or families;
b. communities;
c. educational institutions;
d. child social welfare institutions; and/ore. other institutions engaged in child protection care.

Article 14

1. Handling of children victims of violence as referred to inArticle 11 letter b is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. In handling children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. village government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. communities; and/orh. families.

Article 15

1. Rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 11 letter c are carried out in the form of:a. legal aid services;
b. health rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services; and/ord. repatriation and social reintegration services.
2. In rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with:a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. village government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. communities; and/orh. families.
3. Further provisions regarding the forms and procedures for rehabilitation services for children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 16

1. Every Victim receives the following rights:a. the right to have their dignity respected as a human being; b. the right to health and psychological recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of identity;
f. the right to compensation from the perpetrator;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and/orj. the right to assistance.
2. Children victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), also receive the following special rights:a. the right to respect for the continuity of life, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and/ore. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 21

1. P2TP2A consists of several counseling fields.
2. The counseling fields as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of at least:a. law;
b. health;
c. spiritual; andd. psychology.
3. The implementation of counseling is carried out by counselors and may be implemented in a networked manner by involving related parties.

Article 22

1. The forms of services to Victims organized by P2TP2A include:a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services;
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services;
e. social reintegration services; andf. economic independence services.
2. Further provisions regarding the service mechanism as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 24

The duties and functions of FPKK as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) consist of:
a. coordinating and synchronizing prevention, services, and empowerment of victims of violence against women and children; b. maintaining and developing FPKK in the prevention, services, and empowerment of victims of violence in a networked manner and referral system;
c. conducting education on non-discrimination values towards women and children;
d. disseminating laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence;
e. collecting, compiling, and presenting data reports on victims of violence; andf. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 25

1. The community may participate in the protection of Victims.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:a. provision of safe houses and/or shelters;
b. establishment and management of children's orphanages;
c. establishment of child rehabilitation centers;
d. active supervision of violence against women and children and reporting to the competent authorities;
e. providing first aid to victims;
f. reporting the occurrence of violence against women and children and providing protection;
g. forming family partners at the village level by the community; and h. other forms of community participation related to the implementation of protection for women and protection of children.
